> input from stdin to an entry (JTextField).
> Basically I can't think of how to block in rReadConsole from a  
> JTextField ?.
>

For an example see JGRConsole in JGR. Technically you just use wait()  
[in rReadConsole] and notifyAll() [wherever you have input to  
trigger]. If you want to keep a buffered queue (a good idea), just  
take ConsoleSync from JGR and then it's as simple as calling  
waitForNotification() in rReadConsole and wherever you want to trigger  
the send you call triggerNotification() with the string to pass -  
that's really all.


> (Additionally, I am worried on how to do it right since I have a  
> JavaGD device that I want to keep refreshing)
>
> Specifically, I am confused by who must handle the event loop:
>      - Do you have to block in rReadConsole ?

Yes (block as in don't return but keep Java event loop running).


>      - Can you start the event from a actionPerformed callback ?

Define "event" - you can trigger it from anywhere as described above,  
but you cannot run the R event loop from there.

Cheers,
Simon


