On Jun 12, 2009, at 4:06 , Gerhard Aigner wrote:

> Hi!
>
> We are evaluating some R-tools to play around with biosignals. The  
> "bio signal" is in our case a 100hz captured 3d acceleration over a  
> couple of days. The current version of IPlots looks promising, but  
> when dealing with more than 10000 data points (approx ~3h of data)  
> it starts to get slow and i ran into some java heap overflows. Is it  
> planed to handle such large datasets in the new version of IPlots?

Yes, in the current tests data sizes of 1mio - 10mio data points pose  
no problems (on modern GPUs). The limiting factor is now only the GPU.  
Also a great benefit that data structures are shared with R, i.e. the  
data is never copied so we don't expect memory issues.


> Or will there be a way of implementing different "zoom levels" on  
> data sets?

Possibly, but it's a lesser priority now with the fast performance.


> How long will it take you to release the version after the  
> presentation on useR and DSC ?
>

I expect to release the new iPlots soon after useR/DSC, but even if  
that should become a longer delay, the sources and binary packages  
will be publicly available from RForge.net before the conferences.

Cheers,
Simon

