You'd have to use JRI and initialize Rengine for resize events to work  
otherwise R/JavaGD has no way to call back (see ?.jengine in rJava).


>>> can't find Rengine, automatic resizing disabled. [x:null]
>>> can't find Rengine, automatic resizing disabled. [x:null]
>>> can't find Rengine, automatic resizing disabled. [x:null]
>>> can't find Rengine, automatic resizing disabled. [x:null]
>>> can't find Rengine, automatic resizing disabled. [x:null]
>
> I am not sure how JavaGD deals with the class loader and why it gets
> confused when rJava is loaded after. Something to do with these  
> lines in
> JavaGD :
>
> if ("rJava" %in% .packages() && nchar(.javaGD.get.class.path()) > 0) {
>        .jinit(.javaGD.get.class.path())
>        .javaGD.set.class.path("")
>    }
>
> What about making JavaGD depend on rJava, let rJava deal with the  
> class
> loader from the start and add a call to .jpackage at load time of  
> JavaGD ?
>

JavaGD doesn't require rJava - it works even without it, that's why it  
doesn't "depend" on it. It uses its own JNI code. The messages you see  
are benign - JavaGD is trying various ways to load the JavaGD class.  
(I'm not sure why those messages appear since those exceptions should  
be catched - they don't appear on my system). The code you pointed out  
is there su that rJava is notified of the existence of JavaGD if  
loaded. It's not used when you load them in the JavaGD/rJava order.
However if you plan to use rJava it's better to load it before JavaGD,  
otherwise JavaGD initializes the JVM before rJava, so rJava cannot do  
much of its magic. It will still work, but you can get unpleasant  
issues with the class loader depending on which class loader another  
class etc.

Cheers,
Simon

