Romain,

On Jun 19, 2009, at 4:10 , Romain Francois wrote:

> Would it make sense to have some sort of import functionality in  
> rJava, so that one could do something like this:
>
> .jimport( "javax/swing/*")
> f <- .jnew( "JFrame" )
> f$add( .jnew( "JLabel", "hello world") )
>
> Beanshell does something like this with their NameSpace class
> (http://www.jedit.org/api/org/gjt/sp/jedit/bsh/NameSpace.html)
>
> One important design issue here, would we keep only one such  
> "namespace" of would we tie this with R environments.
>

In fact I was thinking of this after your last contributions ;). There  
are some practical issues (it's easier to allow prefixes instead of  
enumerating packages for classes included), but it's something that  
can be done. However, in order to do this properly, it's not quite  
trivial, because we'd need to hash the names after lookup etc. For the  
reflection API I have no objection at all, because the user expects a  
performance hit, but for the low-level API it's not that clear  
(although it may follow naturally if we first try the low-level  
version and then fall back - but that may have its issues due to  
exception handling..).

Anyway, the real issue is what you mentioned - R packages could be  
tempted to import their classes, so one global namespace may be a bit  
dangerous. But a more elaborate solution is inherently more complex:  
one way would be to look for a  specific variable which will be an  
environment (or Java object) recursively and then on the search path  
such that packages can create one for their own namespace ... but I'm  
not sure how to make this really intuitive ... Probably along the  
lines what S4 does in packages ...

Cheers,
Simon

